+        <div id="engramSettings" class="settingcard"><h3>Project memory · Engram</h3><p>Engram is AdiCode’s searchable notebook for a project. It stores useful facts, decisions and task notes locally, separately from saved chat logs. For example: “The combat cooldown is handled by CombatService.” The AI can look up those notes later instead of starting from scratch.</p><details class="settings-guide"><summary>How to use project memory</summary><p>First select a saved browser chat. Chats assigned to the same project share that project’s notes; unassigned chats keep their own notes. The Memory toggle controls automatic saving of new notes. Existing notes remain available for lookup when automatic saving is off.</p><p><strong>Review project context</strong> shows the notes available to that chat. <strong>Search memory</strong> finds notes by words such as “inventory” or “cooldown”. Search results include a saved fact ID: enter that number to read the full fact or remove it from active memory.</p><p><strong>Add or update a project fact</strong> saves a note manually. Give it a clear title and content. A topic key such as “combat/cooldown” groups updates about the same subject. Saved notes describe past decisions; the AI should still check current scripts before editing.</p><p><strong>Saved chat logs</strong> contain captured messages for review or download. Engram stores project notes, while a continuation file carries recent task context into a new chat. Neither is a game backup or a promise to retain every word of a long conversation.</p></details><div class="instructions-actions"><button class="softbtn" id="engramContext">Review project context</button></div><label for="engramQuery">Find a remembered system or decision</label><input id="engramQuery" type="search" maxlength="1000" placeholder="Combat cooldown, inventory decisions…"><button class="softbtn" id="engramSearch">Search memory</button><div class="instructions-actions"><input id="engramId" type="number" min=